WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.430 --> 00:00:03.160
- I come to Clay County.

00:00:03.160 --> 00:00:06.100
Here where I'm at now,
Manchester, Kentucky.

00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:08.666
Ain't no jobs, nothing.

00:00:08.666 --> 00:00:11.250
And I told the judge
that I said, your honor,

00:00:11.250 --> 00:00:12.530
I said, I wouldn't be this way,

00:00:12.530 --> 00:00:14.930
if you-ins had something for me to do.

00:00:14.930 --> 00:00:15.920
And you know that's true.

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:17.580
That's what's wrong with these kids

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:19.240
today here at Clay County,

00:00:19.240 --> 00:00:21.000
there's nothing for 'em to do.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:24.290
There's no kind of a recreation.

00:00:24.290 --> 00:00:26.270
There's no swimming pool.

00:00:26.270 --> 00:00:27.360
There's no pool room.

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:30.653
Well, there's gambling,
but there's nothing.

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:33.783
There's no kind of no movies.

00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:36.100
Just a little dead town.

00:00:38.330 --> 00:00:40.350
- [Mark] All right Jerry Lee.

00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:41.260
Jerry Lee, where'd you grow up?

00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:42.240
Where are you from originally?

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:44.120
- [Jerry] I'm from Manchester, Kentucky.

00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:45.330
- [Mark] And tell me about your family.

00:00:45.330 --> 00:00:47.290
You had both your parents growing up?

00:00:47.290 --> 00:00:48.730
- Yes.

00:00:48.730 --> 00:00:50.690
- [Mark] How's your childhood?

00:00:50.690 --> 00:00:51.523
- Pretty rough.

00:00:52.730 --> 00:00:54.924
Really, really rough.

00:00:54.924 --> 00:00:56.524
There was a lot of verbal abuse.

00:00:58.337 --> 00:01:00.990
And I guess abuse that where you,

00:01:00.990 --> 00:01:04.963
see your parents hitting one
another, knife stabbings.

00:01:07.990 --> 00:01:10.140
- [Mark] How bad did the violence get?

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:12.372
- It got really bad.

00:01:12.372 --> 00:01:15.970
And I guess it got so
bad, my mom got sick.

00:01:15.970 --> 00:01:18.363
I think that's the reason he took her,

00:01:19.248 --> 00:01:21.773
at that age, because my
dad would throw knives

00:01:21.773 --> 00:01:23.870
that would stick to the back of her head,

00:01:23.870 --> 00:01:26.480
while she was trying
to take up her us kids.

00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:27.530
- [Mark] So your dad?

00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:30.343
- He was really bad jealous.

00:01:31.180 --> 00:01:33.350
- [Mark] So your dad killed your?

00:01:33.350 --> 00:01:35.507
- No, my real mom died of natural causes.

00:01:35.507 --> 00:01:36.490
- [Mark] But your dad
killed your step mom?

00:01:36.490 --> 00:01:37.323
- Yes.

00:01:38.907 --> 00:01:43.907
After my real mom died, he
got remarried to a woman that

00:01:44.190 --> 00:01:45.823
he went to school with.

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:48.803
And then 11 years,

00:01:51.512 --> 00:01:55.138
'til January the 5th, 2005.

00:01:55.138 --> 00:01:57.960
January the 1st, 2005, when it happened,

00:01:57.960 --> 00:01:59.880
at six o'clock in the morning.

00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:00.880
I was going to work.

00:02:01.990 --> 00:02:03.620
I lived in another county.

00:02:03.620 --> 00:02:05.810
I was taking my wife to work,

00:02:05.810 --> 00:02:08.930
and she ran out the door
saying they thought it was me,

00:02:08.930 --> 00:02:10.863
when it was my dad who murdered.

00:02:11.833 --> 00:02:13.410
He was held up in their house.

00:02:13.410 --> 00:02:14.270
I thought he'd killed hisself,

00:02:14.270 --> 00:02:16.023
but he was in there with my mom.

00:02:17.320 --> 00:02:20.523
He kept her in there for two
days before he passed out.

00:02:24.170 --> 00:02:26.550
I don't see how he coulda done that.

00:02:28.790 --> 00:02:32.900
But I think what he did, he
just had so much in his mind,

00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:33.820
where he didn't get help.

00:02:33.820 --> 00:02:37.513
Like, I want help 'cause I
could turn out really bad,

00:02:38.840 --> 00:02:40.040
and I don't wanna go that way.

00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:42.040
I'm gonna choose the good path if I can.

00:02:43.597 --> 00:02:47.050
But daddy, I think he's what changed me,

00:02:47.050 --> 00:02:48.860
made me who I am today,

00:02:48.860 --> 00:02:51.890
because there are people done things,

00:02:51.890 --> 00:02:53.903
I only did was to do good in life,

00:02:54.918 --> 00:02:56.900
'cause it gets harder and it does.

00:02:56.900 --> 00:02:57.733
It's true.

00:02:57.733 --> 00:02:58.566
It gets harder.

00:02:59.660 --> 00:03:01.123
- [Mark] So how far did you go in school?

00:03:01.123 --> 00:03:02.620
- 'Til the eighth.

00:03:02.620 --> 00:03:03.453
- [Mark] Eighth grade?

00:03:03.453 --> 00:03:04.610
- I went into high school,

00:03:04.610 --> 00:03:08.513
but they told me where that you graduated.

00:03:10.499 --> 00:03:12.903
And I said, I graduated
without signing a diploma?

00:03:14.417 --> 00:03:16.280
That was, matter of fact,
just right over there.

00:03:16.280 --> 00:03:18.333
You see the school, Horse Creek.

00:03:20.050 --> 00:03:22.855
- [Mark] And so drugs are very prevalent.

00:03:22.855 --> 00:03:24.034
- Yes, yes they were.

00:03:24.034 --> 00:03:25.560
- [Mark] Like here in
Clay County, Kentucky.

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:27.640
What age did you get started with that?

00:03:27.640 --> 00:03:31.163
- I got started on drugs at probably,

00:03:33.410 --> 00:03:36.890
I'm gonna say seven
years old with marijuana.

00:03:36.890 --> 00:03:39.540
And actually this boy's daddy out here,

00:03:39.540 --> 00:03:42.500
and my uncle Pete, the one-armed guy,

00:03:42.500 --> 00:03:45.721
started marijuana when I was young.

00:03:45.721 --> 00:03:47.600
- [Mark] And then you got
into harder drugs at what age?

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:48.433
- Yes,

00:03:51.168 --> 00:03:52.990
at 15, I think.

00:03:52.990 --> 00:03:55.319
No, 17.

00:03:55.319 --> 00:03:56.236
Pain pills.

00:03:57.496 --> 00:03:58.730
- [Mark] And pain pills
are a big thing out here?

00:03:58.730 --> 00:03:59.563
- Yes.

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:01.710
Well, I'm gonna take that back.

00:04:01.710 --> 00:04:04.810
I was getting a bottle of
Xanax since me 14 years old.

00:04:04.810 --> 00:04:07.813
Went to a doctor and told
him that I needed something,

00:04:07.813 --> 00:04:09.563
like the baby made me nervous.

00:04:10.700 --> 00:04:11.927
I was down on 'em.

00:04:13.685 --> 00:04:15.860
- [Mark] So you had a child at what age?

00:04:15.860 --> 00:04:16.860
- 15.

00:04:16.860 --> 00:04:18.432
- [Mark] 15?

00:04:18.432 --> 00:04:20.599
- He's the same age as Jack Charles,

00:04:20.599 --> 00:04:23.316
that guy in that truck out there.

00:04:23.316 --> 00:04:26.590
There's a small difference in them.

00:04:26.590 --> 00:04:28.980
- [Mark] And how old was it your wife,

00:04:28.980 --> 00:04:31.837
or the mother of the child?

00:04:31.837 --> 00:04:32.670
- The what now?

00:04:32.670 --> 00:04:34.923
- [Mark] The mother of
your child was how old?

00:04:36.288 --> 00:04:39.857
- She was, say, 16 and I was 15.

00:04:39.857 --> 00:04:41.680
- [Mark] Okay, and you guys got married?

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:43.090
- Yeah.

00:04:43.090 --> 00:04:44.923
- [Mark] And how many
children do you have?

00:04:44.923 --> 00:04:46.257
- I got four.

00:04:46.257 --> 00:04:48.707
- [Mark] Four, and you
were around to raise them?

00:04:49.641 --> 00:04:50.542
- Did what?

00:04:50.542 --> 00:04:51.440
- [Mark] Did you raise your kids?

00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:55.450
- No, I did two where I couldn't.

00:04:55.450 --> 00:04:57.253
I tried to raise my kids.

00:04:58.210 --> 00:05:00.900
I was expected to like a regular parent

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:03.633
would the rest of your life,
but I didn't get to do it all,

00:05:03.633 --> 00:05:07.290
because my women I had
was around warrants.

00:05:07.290 --> 00:05:10.163
I tried to raise 'em in a car.

00:05:11.330 --> 00:05:12.750
'Cause when we split up,

00:05:12.750 --> 00:05:14.600
I didn't have nowhere to take 'em to.

00:05:18.100 --> 00:05:20.133
I would stay in a car for a week,

00:05:21.470 --> 00:05:24.190
week at a time, and then I think we'd go

00:05:24.190 --> 00:05:28.710
to a lake in Corbin and
wash off me and my son.

00:05:28.710 --> 00:05:31.870
But he thought we's
camping, but we didn't,

00:05:31.870 --> 00:05:33.780
he didn't know what I was doing.

00:05:33.780 --> 00:05:37.143
He didn't know we didn't have
a home, him and a little dog.

00:05:38.370 --> 00:05:41.263
Now he's 19 years old, he's asking me.

00:05:42.721 --> 00:05:45.253
But my son, he knows when I'm sad.

00:05:47.206 --> 00:05:49.890
'Cause he seen me here not too long ago,

00:05:49.890 --> 00:05:51.610
around one o'clock at night a-crying

00:05:51.610 --> 00:05:53.380
up there at Mike's Pit Stop,

00:05:53.380 --> 00:05:55.933
arguing with the lady that I'm with now.

00:05:56.980 --> 00:06:00.860
He did think I kept
things hidden from him.

00:06:00.860 --> 00:06:03.463
Told him I was happy, but
he caught me that night.

00:06:04.570 --> 00:06:06.070
- [Mark] You get depressed sometimes?

00:06:06.070 --> 00:06:07.563
- Yeah, all the time now.

00:06:08.610 --> 00:06:10.443
It hurts to think,

00:06:14.010 --> 00:06:17.250
why did it happen?

00:06:17.250 --> 00:06:18.760
Why did my daddy do that?

00:06:18.760 --> 00:06:20.460
Why didn't he do things different?

00:06:21.976 --> 00:06:26.197
And I see that there ain't
no jobs here where I'm at,

00:06:26.197 --> 00:06:28.147
and he had to work as a night watchman.

00:06:29.010 --> 00:06:31.353
He done it for 21 years, I think.

00:06:32.938 --> 00:06:34.870
I just seen him when
I come in from school.

00:06:34.870 --> 00:06:39.650
When I was getting up in the mornings,

00:06:39.650 --> 00:06:42.790
they would get us up around 6:00, 6:30.

00:06:42.790 --> 00:06:45.350
I wouldn't have no time
to even speak with him,

00:06:45.350 --> 00:06:46.870
I don't even know my dad.

00:06:46.870 --> 00:06:49.563
I left, I left home at 15 years old.

00:06:52.414 --> 00:06:53.860
Then my mom died.

00:06:53.860 --> 00:06:56.043
- [Mark] What drugs have you done?

00:06:56.043 --> 00:06:58.853
- I've done, maybe everything
they have down here.

00:07:00.696 --> 00:07:05.696
I've done, I guess it'd be
a lower-set hydrocodone.

00:07:06.630 --> 00:07:07.713
Morphine.

00:07:09.620 --> 00:07:11.883
My favorite drug was cocaine.

00:07:15.110 --> 00:07:19.190
I've done everything
that you know of I guess

00:07:19.190 --> 00:07:22.760
except beer, alcohol.

00:07:22.760 --> 00:07:24.130
And I just started drinking it

00:07:24.130 --> 00:07:26.127
until here on the last of 2012.

00:07:27.653 --> 00:07:29.573
And I just got out of prison.

00:07:31.420 --> 00:07:33.510
'Cause I knew my home wasn't the same.

00:07:33.510 --> 00:07:34.343
I could tell.

00:07:35.366 --> 00:07:36.300
And that's when I had a son

00:07:36.300 --> 00:07:39.180
come see me in Louisville,
I stayed at a halfway house.

00:07:39.180 --> 00:07:41.320
That's what helped me, that place.

00:07:41.320 --> 00:07:44.490
It saved my life, saved me
and probably other people,

00:07:44.490 --> 00:07:45.953
'cause I took it serious.

00:07:46.860 --> 00:07:51.180
Because I knew if I
didn't go to this place,

00:07:51.180 --> 00:07:54.310
or tried to get help, I'd never get it.

00:07:54.310 --> 00:07:57.210
But the guy that run the place,

00:07:57.210 --> 00:07:59.613
he was a colored man, his name was Joe.

00:08:00.780 --> 00:08:04.220
To me it was more or less like a court.

00:08:04.220 --> 00:08:05.660
But he said, Jerry you're so quiet,

00:08:05.660 --> 00:08:07.800
I didn't even know you was here.

00:08:07.800 --> 00:08:09.870
'Cause I went up to him on the last week

00:08:09.870 --> 00:08:11.613
of my time there at that place,

00:08:13.431 --> 00:08:18.431
and thanked him for the
classes and helping me realize,

00:08:18.720 --> 00:08:20.670
but he's telling I should've spoken up.

00:08:21.867 --> 00:08:26.530
And that same thing my
dad said, before he died.

00:08:26.530 --> 00:08:27.363
You need to speak up son,

00:08:27.363 --> 00:08:28.980
or people are not gonna know you here.

00:08:28.980 --> 00:08:32.000
So now I do just like they had heard me.

00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:36.350
I argue, I'll argue with
you, I'll fight with you.

00:08:36.350 --> 00:08:38.483
A lot of people thinks I lie.

00:08:39.650 --> 00:08:42.030
And then when I get mad
like that, that I did it

00:08:42.030 --> 00:08:46.653
but I'm just trying to, I
don't have the right reason.

00:08:47.671 --> 00:08:50.110
I guess I take it in the wrong mode

00:08:50.110 --> 00:08:54.190
or something like a real
defensive mode like that.

00:08:54.190 --> 00:08:57.504
I don't know if I said it
wrong to her, but I did.

00:08:57.504 --> 00:08:58.337
It just hurt me.

00:08:58.337 --> 00:08:59.320
- [Mark] And your prison time,

00:08:59.320 --> 00:09:00.920
has been for what kind of stuff?

00:09:01.930 --> 00:09:03.980
- For escapes.

00:09:03.980 --> 00:09:05.443
To escape charges.

00:09:06.400 --> 00:09:07.233
DUIs.

00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:09.833
Possession.

00:09:11.662 --> 00:09:12.995
Assault charges.

00:09:17.259 --> 00:09:19.259
And FTAs and burglaries.

00:09:22.540 --> 00:09:24.445
- [Mark] How far have you
traveled around the country?

00:09:24.445 --> 00:09:25.919
Have you been anywhere?

00:09:25.919 --> 00:09:28.043
- As far as I've been
is Jackson, Mississippi,

00:09:29.909 --> 00:09:30.742
and Georgia,

00:09:33.630 --> 00:09:35.620
and Cincinnati.

00:09:35.620 --> 00:09:37.960
That's as far as I've been.

00:09:40.718 --> 00:09:42.402
I never traveled.

00:09:42.402 --> 00:09:45.940
That's one of my main
goals, though, travel.

00:09:45.940 --> 00:09:47.202
- [Mark] How old are you?

00:09:47.202 --> 00:09:48.035
- 46.

00:09:48.035 --> 00:09:49.035
- [Mark] 46.

00:09:52.000 --> 00:09:53.930
So it's difficult for a young man

00:09:53.930 --> 00:09:56.010
to find work here in Clay County, right?

00:09:56.010 --> 00:09:57.350
- Yes it is.

00:09:57.350 --> 00:09:58.183
- [Mark] You've done pretty good.

00:09:58.183 --> 00:09:59.016
You've had some jobs.

00:09:59.016 --> 00:10:01.720
- No, I never had a job for my life here.

00:10:01.720 --> 00:10:03.200
I've always tried to.

00:10:03.200 --> 00:10:05.806
- [Mark] Yeah, you've
had to travel for work,

00:10:05.806 --> 00:10:07.606
but you made the effort to get work.

00:10:08.605 --> 00:10:09.700
- But it was hard.

00:10:09.700 --> 00:10:11.290
- [Interviewer] But to
stay here in Clay County,

00:10:11.290 --> 00:10:12.250
as you've chosen to do,

00:10:12.250 --> 00:10:14.760
makes your life very difficult, right?

00:10:14.760 --> 00:10:16.720
- Really, really, really hard.

00:10:16.720 --> 00:10:18.490
- [Mark] Because you're
surrounded by what?

00:10:18.490 --> 00:10:19.323
- Drugs.

00:10:22.580 --> 00:10:23.743
I'm surrounded by.

00:10:24.700 --> 00:10:25.710
- [Mark] And you choose to stay here

00:10:25.710 --> 00:10:27.010
for what reason you think?

00:10:31.800 --> 00:10:33.812
- I will say, why I'm
choosing to stay here

00:10:33.812 --> 00:10:36.150
is 'cause of my little brother.

00:10:36.150 --> 00:10:37.800
So when I was telling
him out there at the end,

00:10:37.800 --> 00:10:41.600
I said I'm leaving here if I can,

00:10:41.600 --> 00:10:44.593
I gotta get some kind of
support to do it though.

00:10:46.210 --> 00:10:48.143
- [Mark] And are drugs still
a problem for you today?

00:10:49.080 --> 00:10:50.340
- Yeah.

00:10:50.340 --> 00:10:51.630
- [Mark] What are you using today?

00:10:51.630 --> 00:10:54.470
- I use Suboxone and Neurontin,

00:10:54.470 --> 00:10:56.963
and I use marijuana sometimes to sleep.

00:10:58.490 --> 00:10:59.890
But the Suboxone I think,

00:10:59.890 --> 00:11:01.915
is no different from any other drug.

00:11:01.915 --> 00:11:04.690
It just takes the place of another drug.

00:11:04.690 --> 00:11:07.393
But it will help you, if you use it right,

00:11:09.096 --> 00:11:11.750
and it is prescribed.

00:11:11.750 --> 00:11:14.250
And it took me years to get it prescribed.

00:11:14.250 --> 00:11:17.210
I spent every dime I had
to getting that drug,

00:11:17.210 --> 00:11:19.640
having to buy it off the street.

00:11:19.640 --> 00:11:21.910
You know, I finally got me
a doctor now it's my chance.

00:11:21.910 --> 00:11:26.080
If I could get me another
job, I could save my money.

00:11:26.080 --> 00:11:28.270
I had to spend it on what

00:11:28.270 --> 00:11:30.750
I used to call my habit every day.

00:11:30.750 --> 00:11:33.010
I went and picked tens up.

00:11:33.010 --> 00:11:35.090
When I moved to Clay County.

00:11:35.090 --> 00:11:37.553
When I left Somerset, I was working,

00:11:38.860 --> 00:11:40.700
and coming to Clay County,

00:11:40.700 --> 00:11:43.160
here where I'm at now,
Manchester, Kentucky.

00:11:43.160 --> 00:11:45.510
Ain't no jobs, nothing.

00:11:45.510 --> 00:11:48.920
And I told the judge,
that, I said, your honor,

00:11:48.920 --> 00:11:50.060
I said, I wouldn't be this way

00:11:50.060 --> 00:11:52.460
if you-ins had something for me to do.

00:11:52.460 --> 00:11:53.460
And you know it's true.

00:11:53.460 --> 00:11:55.878
That's what's wrong with these kids

00:11:55.878 --> 00:11:56.790
today here in Clay County,

00:11:56.790 --> 00:11:58.340
there's nothing for them to do.

00:11:59.303 --> 00:12:01.820
There's no kind of a recreation,

00:12:01.820 --> 00:12:03.810
there's no swimming pool,

00:12:03.810 --> 00:12:04.863
there's no pool room.

00:12:05.716 --> 00:12:07.250
Well, there's gambling.

00:12:07.250 --> 00:12:08.250
But there's nothing.

00:12:09.370 --> 00:12:11.343
There's no kind of no movies.

00:12:12.450 --> 00:12:13.940
Just a little dead town.

00:12:13.940 --> 00:12:17.400
Used to be out here lively back then,

00:12:17.400 --> 00:12:20.930
had a little drive-in,
now they shut it down.

00:12:20.930 --> 00:12:22.650
- [Mark] Do you have any friends here?

00:12:22.650 --> 00:12:23.600
- They're all dead.

00:12:27.548 --> 00:12:30.600
I have friends I got or did have.

00:12:30.600 --> 00:12:33.490
That's why I was gonna
tell you about me going.

00:12:33.490 --> 00:12:36.670
If I had went with them, they chose,

00:12:36.670 --> 00:12:38.990
I guess not working things
and that's what saved me.

00:12:38.990 --> 00:12:40.140
I had a job.

00:12:40.140 --> 00:12:43.470
There's a few times if I'd
went with those friends,

00:12:43.470 --> 00:12:47.200
I wouldn't be here today, talking,

00:12:47.200 --> 00:12:50.760
'cause the same car, the same boys

00:12:50.760 --> 00:12:53.290
I knew went to school with these boys.

00:12:53.290 --> 00:12:55.400
Took my best friend out and shot him

00:12:55.400 --> 00:12:57.550
in the back, killed him.

00:12:57.550 --> 00:12:59.050
They wanted me to go with him.

00:13:00.126 --> 00:13:02.233
And if I'd went the next day,

00:13:04.540 --> 00:13:07.740
I would have been killed,
they'd found me shot.

00:13:07.740 --> 00:13:08.963
They came after me.

00:13:10.820 --> 00:13:15.050
I think I was 17, 18 years old.

00:13:15.050 --> 00:13:18.460
Now this boy got out here a few years.

00:13:18.460 --> 00:13:22.410
Last year, or two year ago, I'm gonna say.

00:13:22.410 --> 00:13:26.357
He done life since 20 years.

00:13:26.357 --> 00:13:31.357
He's the same age I am,
and he's done 25 years.

00:13:31.630 --> 00:13:32.793
I couldn't do that now.

00:13:33.780 --> 00:13:35.080
Too old.

00:13:35.080 --> 00:13:37.305
- [Mark] What are your children doing?

00:13:37.305 --> 00:13:38.138
- What do they do?

00:13:38.138 --> 00:13:38.971
- [Mark] What are they doing?

00:13:38.971 --> 00:13:39.804
How old are they now?

00:13:40.760 --> 00:13:44.023
- My oldest child, I think is 29.

00:13:45.540 --> 00:13:48.440
And my oldest girl, is I'm gonna say 20.

00:13:49.677 --> 00:13:51.914
My oldest boy is 19.

00:13:51.914 --> 00:13:55.290
And my youngest girl is,

00:13:56.240 --> 00:13:57.073
I think 11.

00:13:58.349 --> 00:14:00.393
- [Mark] And they're still
staying here in Clay County?

00:14:01.640 --> 00:14:04.483
- Two of them are, two are
gone in other counties.

00:14:05.400 --> 00:14:07.630
I got a girl that lives in Corbin.

00:14:07.630 --> 00:14:08.923
She's in a foster home.

00:14:10.070 --> 00:14:12.597
And my boy is getting ready to be a daddy,

00:14:12.597 --> 00:14:13.880
and I'm going to be a pawpaw.

00:14:13.880 --> 00:14:15.940
That's one thing that's made me,

00:14:15.940 --> 00:14:18.440
I got to get myself straightened out,

00:14:18.440 --> 00:14:20.363
because I want to be an example.

00:14:21.300 --> 00:14:24.800
I want to be able to tell
them, to try to guide them

00:14:24.800 --> 00:14:27.480
in the wrong way or the right way.

00:14:27.480 --> 00:14:29.610
You know, take that wrong path.

00:14:29.610 --> 00:14:32.310
Don't want 'em to be nothing like I am.

00:14:32.310 --> 00:14:33.812
- [Mark] You still talk to your kids?

00:14:33.812 --> 00:14:34.880
- Yeah.

00:14:34.880 --> 00:14:39.053
But that is just I think
when they want some money.

00:14:41.904 --> 00:14:43.570
I do talk to my kids.

00:14:46.200 --> 00:14:47.410
I tried to talk to my oldest boy,

00:14:47.410 --> 00:14:49.723
the one that's getting
ready to be a daddy.

00:14:53.336 --> 00:14:56.670
He said he wanted to be
a killer like his papaw.

00:14:56.670 --> 00:14:57.870
His name is Christopher,

00:14:59.210 --> 00:15:01.560
and it breaks my heart when he says that.

00:15:01.560 --> 00:15:02.393
- [Mark] He says what?

00:15:02.393 --> 00:15:04.620
- He wants to be a killer.

00:15:04.620 --> 00:15:05.680
- [Mark] Like your dad?

00:15:05.680 --> 00:15:07.443
- Like his papaw, yeah, my dad.

00:15:11.810 --> 00:15:13.440
But I think they got him

00:15:13.440 --> 00:15:16.853
some kind of help, and he's straightened.

00:15:18.130 --> 00:15:19.080
He ain't no killer.

00:15:20.360 --> 00:15:21.193
Hope not.

00:15:22.483 --> 00:15:25.403
But he got him a job, when he quit school.

00:15:30.805 --> 00:15:33.780
This happened maybe two months ago.

00:15:33.780 --> 00:15:36.380
I ain't got no way of calling
him but I use Facebook,

00:15:36.380 --> 00:15:37.727
and I was trying to explain to him

00:15:37.727 --> 00:15:39.393
'cause he's going to be a dad.

00:15:41.939 --> 00:15:45.683
Go 'em to school while
they can, or home-bound.

00:15:47.768 --> 00:15:48.630
I just told my story.

00:15:48.630 --> 00:15:50.130
I told him some of it,

00:15:50.130 --> 00:15:52.180
but his girlfriend took it the wrong way.

00:15:57.310 --> 00:15:58.330
She took it the wrong way.

00:15:58.330 --> 00:16:01.160
I guess I said it not be to like I was.

00:16:01.160 --> 00:16:02.683
I chose the wrong path.

00:16:05.305 --> 00:16:06.177
I chose lower sets and stuff like that,

00:16:06.177 --> 00:16:07.760
and she thought I was doing it now,

00:16:07.760 --> 00:16:12.303
but I was trying to tell
my story, like I was here.

00:16:13.420 --> 00:16:15.320
And we got into it and she blocked me.

00:16:16.170 --> 00:16:19.790
But he told me, he said, dad
she just took it the wrong way.

00:16:19.790 --> 00:16:22.280
Well, what I was trying to
do is to just use myself

00:16:22.280 --> 00:16:24.570
as an example and describe how I did it,

00:16:24.570 --> 00:16:29.570
but she thought I was talking
about the future now I guess.

00:16:29.706 --> 00:16:31.356
But I was talking about the past.

00:16:32.800 --> 00:16:35.060
That it destroyed my life.

00:16:35.060 --> 00:16:36.810
That I chose the wrong road.

00:16:36.810 --> 00:16:39.373
I chose to hung out with wrong friends,

00:16:40.280 --> 00:16:43.123
and I had caused me to be
that way, that I am now.

00:16:45.700 --> 00:16:47.433
- [Mark] And what's your biggest regret?

00:16:50.400 --> 00:16:51.653
- Not finishing school.

00:16:52.857 --> 00:16:56.190
I could have had so many good jobs,

00:16:56.190 --> 00:16:58.260
if I would have finished school.

00:16:58.260 --> 00:17:01.283
My education, the kind of work I do now.

00:17:02.220 --> 00:17:04.910
I always wanted to be like an electrician

00:17:05.892 --> 00:17:09.440
or a lineman, a good lineman,

00:17:09.440 --> 00:17:12.750
is the best thing like coal
mining that paid top dollar,

00:17:12.750 --> 00:17:15.650
because it was dangerous to
work for the electric company.

00:17:16.739 --> 00:17:18.526
But you had to have a high school diploma,

00:17:18.526 --> 00:17:19.626
and I couldn't get it.

00:17:20.750 --> 00:17:23.953
If I had finished school,
I'd been set right now.

00:17:26.866 --> 00:17:27.699
- [Mark] Do you regret not being

00:17:27.699 --> 00:17:29.280
a better role model for your kids?

00:17:29.280 --> 00:17:30.903
- Yes I do,

00:17:31.813 --> 00:17:33.973
'cause if my dad been there for me,

00:17:35.179 --> 00:17:36.803
and been more provided,

00:17:39.941 --> 00:17:40.920
or a role model for me.

00:17:40.920 --> 00:17:41.903
I didn't see my daddy.

00:17:41.903 --> 00:17:43.960
I had nobody to be a role model.

00:17:43.960 --> 00:17:47.800
I had to learn it all on my
own every day, every step.

00:17:47.800 --> 00:17:49.230
It was so hard.

00:17:49.230 --> 00:17:52.130
Every mistake I made, I paid for it.

00:17:52.130 --> 00:17:54.750
But that next week,
when I started back in,

00:17:54.750 --> 00:17:56.930
I wasn't gonna go back and do that again.

00:17:56.930 --> 00:17:58.680
But I got further and further.

00:17:58.680 --> 00:18:01.870
You're around 46 going
on almost 50 years old.

00:18:01.870 --> 00:18:03.570
That's hard to believe.

00:18:03.570 --> 00:18:06.840
Then all my friends didn't think that way.

00:18:06.840 --> 00:18:08.560
They chose the wrong path.

00:18:08.560 --> 00:18:10.520
I don't have a friend now.

00:18:10.520 --> 00:18:12.420
Might be a few on this side.

00:18:12.420 --> 00:18:14.663
They moved away or something,

00:18:14.663 --> 00:18:18.710
but near Clay County ain't nobody I have.

00:18:18.710 --> 00:18:22.510
Just wish I went farther
in school and done right.

00:18:22.510 --> 00:18:23.843
I regret it.

00:18:24.750 --> 00:18:26.890
- [Mark] How much do you
credit your environment

00:18:28.517 --> 00:18:31.117
versus your family for the
path your life has taken?

00:18:39.057 --> 00:18:42.520
Do you think it's the family
that you had around you,

00:18:42.520 --> 00:18:44.393
the role models, or is it the fact

00:18:44.393 --> 00:18:46.393
that you grew up in here in Clay County?

00:18:48.310 --> 00:18:52.356
- One of my doctors told me
its the environment I'm in.

00:18:52.356 --> 00:18:53.189
It's what they tell me that

00:18:53.189 --> 00:18:56.163
I could take the drugs
that I take now to help me.

00:18:58.067 --> 00:19:00.490
It's the environment,
the people I'm around.

00:19:00.490 --> 00:19:04.500
And where I go is nothing but violence.

00:19:04.500 --> 00:19:05.443
And where you go?

00:19:07.206 --> 00:19:09.810
I don't have nowhere else
to run or hide no more.

00:19:09.810 --> 00:19:14.810
So I got to take this to heart
and learn and do it fast.

00:19:15.920 --> 00:19:18.913
Because time is going
on, you can't stop it.

00:19:19.980 --> 00:19:22.880
Every mistake I made,
I won't do it no more.

00:19:22.880 --> 00:19:24.463
Tired, I don't want to go jail.

00:19:25.454 --> 00:19:29.650
I'm praying about knowing
that I feel strange,

00:19:29.650 --> 00:19:31.023
like something's wrong,

00:19:32.090 --> 00:19:33.990
and I'd probably be in jail next week.

00:19:35.860 --> 00:19:38.393
Got this girl and she's
crazy as hell over me.

00:19:39.820 --> 00:19:41.270
She never had nobody like me.

00:19:41.270 --> 00:19:44.373
But she shouldn't be so jealous.

00:19:45.482 --> 00:19:48.810
If you don't have trust,
you don't have nothing, man.

00:19:48.810 --> 00:19:50.883
I'm honest to her.

00:19:52.340 --> 00:19:56.970
I've been married three
times and I've give each one

00:19:56.970 --> 00:20:00.663
of them women something
very special, a baby.

00:20:02.010 --> 00:20:04.260
And they took it away from me.

00:20:04.260 --> 00:20:05.980
They'll split us up apart from me,

00:20:05.980 --> 00:20:07.600
from being a dad to 'em,

00:20:07.600 --> 00:20:12.020
something that was my
lifeline to for me to do good,

00:20:12.020 --> 00:20:16.100
because I wanted to do for
them kids that I wanted

00:20:16.100 --> 00:20:18.010
when I was growing up.

00:20:18.010 --> 00:20:20.540
I see these kids in school.

00:20:20.540 --> 00:20:23.803
How did they get to
have a Mustang or that?

00:20:25.076 --> 00:20:27.310
Or had a new home?

00:20:27.310 --> 00:20:29.150
I wanted be able to
tell them how to do it,

00:20:29.150 --> 00:20:32.090
and what it takes to get that goal you got

00:20:32.090 --> 00:20:35.680
to have the will and the heart to do it.

00:20:35.680 --> 00:20:37.063
And I had nobody to help me.

00:20:40.655 --> 00:20:41.605
But, it's been bad.

00:20:45.682 --> 00:20:47.093
I can go back in time.

00:20:47.093 --> 00:20:47.926
I don't know.

00:20:47.926 --> 00:20:48.759
I can change the subject,

00:20:48.759 --> 00:20:50.750
and go back to what really hurt me a lot.

00:20:51.841 --> 00:20:52.710
I would play football,

00:20:52.710 --> 00:20:55.300
I was the best football
player here in Clay County.

00:20:55.300 --> 00:20:57.463
I won the championship for this school.

00:21:00.810 --> 00:21:03.390
I could get in trouble and not
even have to worry about it.

00:21:03.390 --> 00:21:05.150
I said principal's out in life.

00:21:05.150 --> 00:21:08.963
Then I changed school and
the bus driver took me home.

00:21:10.060 --> 00:21:11.450
I see it's happened to a lot of kids,

00:21:11.450 --> 00:21:12.707
I ain't ashamed to say it.

00:21:12.707 --> 00:21:13.903
But it's hurt me.

00:21:14.810 --> 00:21:19.810
You know, my daddy verbally hurt me bad,

00:21:19.830 --> 00:21:23.707
and abusive I guess, with his gun belts.

00:21:23.707 --> 00:21:25.143
But my bus driver,

00:21:25.986 --> 00:21:27.130
and there's probably other little kids

00:21:27.130 --> 00:21:29.313
just not spoke up and said about it.

00:21:30.701 --> 00:21:34.371
I guess the reason my
momma and daddy let me go,

00:21:34.371 --> 00:21:36.310
they trusted that bus driver.

00:21:36.310 --> 00:21:39.093
But I told my mom, I didn't tell my daddy.

00:21:40.090 --> 00:21:42.183
She knew, but she was afraid to tell him.

00:21:43.049 --> 00:21:45.070
- [Mark] What happened
with the bus driver?

00:21:45.070 --> 00:21:46.560
- He took me home with him.

00:21:46.560 --> 00:21:49.460
Let me ride little horses, little ponies.

00:21:49.460 --> 00:21:50.533
He had race horses.

00:21:51.912 --> 00:21:55.611
Then it came eating time and laid down,

00:21:55.611 --> 00:21:56.787
and wanted me to get into bed with him,

00:21:56.787 --> 00:21:58.893
and he touched me and stuff.

00:22:02.634 --> 00:22:03.467
I was 13.

00:22:04.473 --> 00:22:08.510
And like I said, 14, 15, I was married.

00:22:08.510 --> 00:22:10.070
And I didn't say that when I go work,

00:22:10.070 --> 00:22:12.993
I think that people knew
I was ashamed of myself.

00:22:12.993 --> 00:22:14.460
I could get so mad.

00:22:14.460 --> 00:22:15.930
I've worked so hard.

00:22:15.930 --> 00:22:16.883
I'd work harder.

00:22:18.190 --> 00:22:19.440
He hurt me.

00:22:19.440 --> 00:22:20.273
He broke.

00:22:21.925 --> 00:22:24.763
I thought of it every day, every
day of my life pretty much.

00:22:28.020 --> 00:22:29.630
But, I'm not ashamed,

00:22:29.630 --> 00:22:32.073
I should have spoke out a long time ago.

00:22:32.073 --> 00:22:33.523
Now just look what he's done.

00:22:35.341 --> 00:22:40.060
Well, I didn't get to ask
for help 'til it's too late.

00:22:40.990 --> 00:22:42.260
It's not too late.

00:22:42.260 --> 00:22:43.430
Oh, it's not.

00:22:43.430 --> 00:22:44.623
- [Mark] It's never too late.

00:22:45.720 --> 00:22:46.700
- [Jerry] 'Cause there's so much

00:22:46.700 --> 00:22:49.473
I want to say to anybody and everybody.

00:22:51.260 --> 00:22:52.120
- [Mark] All right, Jerry.

00:22:52.120 --> 00:22:53.120
Thank you very much.

